19:T5ab,## A million near-identical objects

Sometimes a program needs a *staggering* number of objects that are almost the same, a forest of a million trees, every glyph on a page, every particle in an explosion. If each one carries its own copy of the heavy data, you run out of memory long before you run out of trees.

## Intrinsic vs extrinsic state

The whole pattern hinges on one split:

- **Intrinsic** state, shared, context-independent. A tree's **mesh and texture**: the same for every oak.
- **Extrinsic** state, unique per instance, passed in. A tree's **x, y position**: different for each one.

Store the intrinsic state **once** inside a shared **flyweight** that many objects reference. Pass the extrinsic state in at the moment you need it (e.g. `draw(x, y)`).

```
class TreeType {            // the flyweight: intrinsic, shared
    Mesh mesh; Texture texture;
    void draw(int x, int y) { ... }   // extrinsic passed in
}
class Tree { int x, y; TreeType type; }   // tiny: just a position + a reference
```

## The factory pools them

A **factory** keeps a pool of flyweights and reuses identical ones instead of recreating them, ask for the `"oak"` type and you always get the **same** `TreeType` instance back.

> Flyweights must be **immutable**, they're shared, so mutating one would corrupt every object that references it. Render a million trees that share a handful of `TreeTypes` and memory drops from **gigabytes to megabytes**.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-structural","activeLesson":"flyweight"}],["$","$L18","flyweight",{"lesson":{"id":"flyweight","patternId":"lld-structural","title":"Flyweight","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Advanced","statement":"When you need a HUGE number of similar objects, share their common heavy data instead of duplicating it, keep only the per-object differences.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Unity"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"A million heavy objects","code":["class Tree {","    int x, y;","    Mesh mesh;        // 2 MB","    Texture texture;  // 3 MB, copied into EVERY tree","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Each Tree carries its own mesh and texture: 5 MB of heavy geometry baked right into the object alongside its x, y.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"tree","name":"Tree","fields":["- x, y","- mesh (2 MB)","- texture (3 MB)"],"x":1,"y":0.4,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[],"shownClassIds":["tree"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["tree"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Now render a forest. A million trees means a million copies of the SAME 5 MB mesh and texture, gigabytes wasted on data that never actually differs.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["tree"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["tree"]},"vars":[["1M trees","~5 TB 💥"]]}]},{"label":"Share the heavy part","code":["class TreeType {                         // the flyweight, intrinsic","    Mesh mesh; Texture texture;          // heavy, shared","    void draw(int x, int y) { ... }      // extrinsic passed in","}","class Tree { int x, y; TreeType type; }  // tiny, just a reference","class TreeFactory {","    Map<String,TreeType> pool;","    TreeType getType(String name) { ... } // reuse, never recreate","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Split the Tree: keep only the per-tree x, y. Move the heavy mesh + texture into a TreeType, the flyweight that holds the intrinsic, shared state.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"tree","name":"Tree","fields":["- x, y","- type: TreeType"],"methods":["+ draw()"],"x":0,"y":0.4},{"id":"type","name":"TreeType","fields":["- mesh (2 MB)","- texture (3 MB)"],"methods":["+ draw(x,y)"],"x":1.6,"y":0.4,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"factory","name":"TreeFactory","methods":["+ getType(name)"],"x":1.6,"y":1.7,"tint":"accent2"}],"relations":[{"id":"r1","from":"tree","to":"type","kind":"aggregate","label":"shared"},{"id":"r2","from":"factory","to":"type","kind":"depend","label":"pools"}],"shownClassIds":["tree","type"],"shownRelIds":["r1"],"activeIds":["type"]},"vars":[["intrinsic","mesh + texture"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"Every Tree just REFERENCES a shared TreeType. A million trees, but only a few TreeTypes ever live in memory.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["tree","type"],"shownRelIds":["r1"],"activeIds":["tree"]},"vars":[["extrinsic","x, y per tree"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":7,"narration":"The TreeFactory pools the flyweights. The Forest asks for an \"oak\" type, first time, it doesn't exist yet, so the factory CREATES it once.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"c","label":"Forest","kind":"object"},{"id":"f","label":"TreeFactory","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"t","label":"TreeType"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"c","to":"f","label":"getType(\"oak\")","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"f","to":"t","label":"«create» oak type","kind":"create"},{"id":"m3","from":"f","to":"c","label":"oak type","kind":"return"},{"id":"m4","from":"c","to":"f","label":"getType(\"oak\")  again","kind":"call"},{"id":"m5","from":"f","to":"c","label":"same oak type","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":3,"activeIndex":1},"classDiagram":"$undefined","vars":[["oak type","created once"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":7,"narration":"Ask for \"oak\" again and the factory hands back the SAME instance, no second mesh, no second texture. Intrinsic state shared via the flyweight, extrinsic x, y passed at draw time → memory drops from gigabytes to megabytes.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":5,"activeIndex":4},"vars":[["1M trees","a few TreeTypes ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
